SELECT ivc.id, 'en', 'Hello everyone. Welcome to Nesso Academy. In the previous lecture, we have understood what is a machine learning model. And now, from this lecture onwards, we are going to understand some important terminologies in machine learning, which will be used frequently in this course. This is the very first lecture on important terminologies of machine learning. And the name of the lecture is data set fundamentals. In this lecture, we are going to understand about four important terms related to data set in machine learning. So, the topic for this lecture is data set fundamentals. Let''s get started. As you can see, the very first term inside the data set fundamentals is data set itself. So, what is a data set? Data set is the collection of data examples used to train and test an ML model. What does that mean? It simply means that data set is simply the collection of related data examples that are used for training as well as testing of a machine learning model. Like in the previous lecture, we had the data set of multiple images of cats and dogs that we have used to train and test our image classification model. So, that was the data set. That is why we can say data set is the collection of all the data examples that we have. And those data examples are used for training as well as testing of our machine learning model. For example, a house price data set containing information such as size, location, age, and price of different houses. Suppose we have data of different houses, like size of the house, location of the house, age of the house, and prices of the house. Now, the collection of all these data will form a house price data set. So, this is the house price data set that we have. And in this particular data set, you can see there are multiple columns. The first column is house ID. House ID basically means the house number. House number one, house number two, house number three, and so on. Size sq ft means the size of the house in square feet. 1800 sq ft, 2200 sq ft, 1500 sq ft, and so on. Bedrooms means the number of bedrooms in the house. Like house number one has three bedrooms, house number two has four bedrooms, house number three has three bedrooms, and so on. Now, what is location? Location means where the house is located. Like house number one is located at Manhattan, house number two is located at Tokyo, house number three is located at London, and so on. Age years means the age of the house in years. That means how old the house is. House number one is 5 years old. House number two is minus 3 years old. House number three is 12 years old, and so on. Now, garage means whether the house has a garage or not. House number one has a garage? Yes. House number two has a garage? Yes. House number three has a garage? No. And so on. Now, finally price USD means the price of the house in US dollars. Like for house number one, the price is 950,000 US dollars. So, this is the example of a house price data set. Now, if you observe carefully in this particular data set, there are many errors. Like, the size of the house cannot be zero. The size of the house cannot be negative, also. There is a missing value in the bedrooms column. Also, if we see, age in years cannot be negative. Also, in this column, that is price USD, this value is very large as compared to other values, which is not practically possible. So, these are some errors in our house price data set. So, that means it is not necessary that we always get a perfect data set. There can be some errors in our data set. And these errors we have to fix. We generally fix these errors during the data preprocessing and data cleaning stages. We will learn more about data cleaning and data preprocessing later on. So, I hope you now understand what is a data set. Let''s move towards the second term under data set fundamentals, that is instance or sample. Now, what is an instance? A single data example in a data set is called instance. So, in this particular data set, you can see we have multiple data examples. This entire row is a data example. This entire row is also a data example. This entire row is also a data example, and so on. That means we have multiple data examples in this particular data set. So, this means that individually all the data examples, or we can say all the rows, are the instances of this data set. For example, information about one house in a house price data set is an instance. If we consider any one house at a time, then the information about that particular house will be the instance of the data set. Suppose we take the house number two, then this entire row, or we can say this entire data example, will be one particular instance of this data set. And this instance is described by these features. House number two is the house ID. 2200 is the size of the house. Four is the number of bedrooms in the house. Tokyo is the location of the house, and so on. That means house ID, size sq ft, bedrooms, location, age years, garage, are all the features of the house. So, this is how we have understood instance or sample of a data set. Now, we will move towards the third term inside the data set fundamentals, which is feature or attribute. Now, what is a feature? An input variable or characteristic that describes an instance and is used by the model for learning. What does that mean? It means that feature is an input variable. Now, what is input variable? Input variable is the data that is given to the machine learning model as an input. So, feature is the data that is given to the machine learning model as input. Feature is the characteristic that describes describes an instance. We have just understood that features are the characteristics that describe an instance. Like, these all are the instances of this data set, and these are the features that are used to describe an instance. So, the houses are described by the features like house ID, size sq ft, bedrooms, location, age years, and garage. Now, feature is used by the model for learning. That means during the training process of a machine learning model, the model learns the features. For example, size, location, age, etc. of the house are the features of the house. That means house ID, size sq ft, bedrooms, location, age years, and garage are all the features of the houses. Now, why price USD is not the feature of the house? Because as per the definition, feature is an input variable. But price USD is an output variable. In this particular machine learning problem, that is house price prediction, the price of the house is to be predicted by the machine learning model. That means price USD is the output variable. It is not the input variable. That is why only these are the features of the houses. I hope you have understood what is a feature or an attribute. Now, let''s move towards the fourth term, that is label or target variable. Label is the output variable that the model is expected to predict. Now, this label or the target variable is the output variable that the model is expected to predict. That means in this house price data set, this price USD is the output variable, that is the label or the target variable. Because our model will predict the price of the house on the basis of these features of the house. For example, the price of the house, as we have just understood, the price of the house in this particular data set is the output variable. That is why the price USD is the label or the target variable in this particular data set. And in this way, we are done with the label or target variable as well. So, we have understood total four terms related to data set in machine learning. Data set itself, instance, feature, and target variable. So, in this way, we are done with our topic data set fundamentals. This was all from my side for this lecture. Thank you, and I''ll see you in the next lecture. >> [music] [music]', '[{"start": 0.0, "end": 5.0, "text": "Hello everyone. Welcome to Nesso\nAcademy. In the p