00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
by its alternative names like designing

13
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.000
a machine learning system or steps to

14
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.000
build a machine learning model. So the

15
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.000
topic for this lecture is

16
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
machine learning pipeline. Let''s get

17
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.000
started.

18
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Machine learning pipeline is a

19
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
step-by-step process followed to build a

20
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
machine learning model.

21
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.000
What does that mean?

22
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.000
It means

23
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
to build a machine learning model, we

24
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.000
need to follow a step-by-step process.

25
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
And that step-by-step process is known

26
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
as machine learning pipeline.

27
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
For example, when we cook food, we

28
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
follow some steps. When we solve a

29
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.000
mathematical problem, we follow some

30
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
steps. Similarly, in machine learning

31
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
also, we follow a proper sequence of

32
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
steps to build a machine learning model.

33
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.000
We don''t directly give data to the

34
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
machine learning model.

35
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.000
First, we need to understand the problem

36
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
statement. Then we need to collect the

37
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.000
data relevant to that problem. Then we

38
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.000
have to clean and prepare that data.

39
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.000
After that, we train our machine

40
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
learning model. Then we test our machine

41
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.000
learning model. And then we can use the

42
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.000
machine learning model to make

43
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
predictions. That means we cannot use

44
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.000
machine learning model directly for

45
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.000
making predictions.

46
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.000
First, we need to follow some steps. And

47
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.000
after completion of all the steps, then

48
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
we can expect our machine learning model

49
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000
to make predictions about the data.

50
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.000
And in this lecture, we are going to

51
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
understand the flow of complete machine

52
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.000
learning pipeline.

53
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.000
From the upcoming lectures, we will

54
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.000
understand each of the steps of this ML

55
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
pipeline in much more details. So, the

56
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.000
very first step in machine learning

57
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.000
pipeline is

58
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.000
problem definition.

59
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Yes. Problem definition is the very

60
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.000
first step in building a machine

61
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.000
learning model.

62
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.000
That means, before building a machine

63
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.000
learning model, we must clearly

64
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.000
understand the problem statement that we

65
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.000
are trying to solve.

66
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
This is a very important step because if

67
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.000
problem is not clear, then the whole

68
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.000
machine learning process will go in

69
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.000
wrong direction.

70
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.000
For example, in house price prediction,

71
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
what is the problem statement?

72
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Obviously, the problem statement is to

73
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.000
predict the prices of the houses on the

74
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.000
basis of features of the houses, like

75
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.000
house size, number of bedrooms, age of

76
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.000
the house, location of the house, and so

77
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.000
on.

78
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
So, in the first step, we define problem

79
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.000
statement, and we also identify the

80
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
three components of a well-defined

81
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.000
learning problem, that are

82
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
performance measure, experience, and

83
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.000
task.

84
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.000
So, after defining the problem

85
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.000
statement, the second step is

86
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.000
data collection.

87
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Obviously, after defining the problem

88
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.000
statement, we need to collect data for

89
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.000
the machine learning model because

90
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.000
machine learning model learns patterns,

91
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.000
features, and relationships from the

92
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.000
data. That is why we collect data from

93
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.000
different sources.

94
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000
What can be the different sources?

95
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Like companies'' databases, surveys,

96
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.000
sensors, and so on.

97
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Now, in this particular step, that is

98
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
data collection, the quality of data

99
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
influence directly the performance of

100
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.000
the machine learning model. That means

101
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.000
if the quality of data is very good,

102
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
that means the performance of the

103
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.000
machine learning model is also very

104
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.000
good. But, if the quality of data is

105
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.000
bad, then the performance of machine

106
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.000
learning model will also be very poor.

107
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.000
So, the data that we need to collect

108
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.000
should be relevant to the problem

109
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
statement.

110
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Machine learning model cannot learn from

111
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.000
less or incomplete data.

112
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.000
So, I hope second step is now clear to

113
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.000
you.

114
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.000
After data collection, tell me, should

115
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.000
we give this data directly to our

116
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
machine learning model?

117
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.000
No, not at all.

118
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.000
First, we need to remove some problems

119
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.000
from this data. Because the raw data

120
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.000
that we collect is very messy,

121
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.000
unstructured, unformatted, and

122
00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.000
inconsistent. That is why we need to

123
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.000
perform basic data cleaning. So, the

124
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.000
third step of machine learning pipeline

125
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.000
is basic data cleaning.

126
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.000
What do you mean by basic data cleaning?

127
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.000
It means to remove some obvious problems

128
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.000
or errors from the data that we have

129
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
collected. Now, what can be the problems

130
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.000
in this data set? There can be some

131
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.000
duplicate rows. There can be some

132
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.000
irrelevant columns. There can be some

133
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.000
missing values. So, all such problems

134
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.000
are handled in this particular step,

135
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.000
that is basic data cleaning. For

136
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.000
example, in case of house price data

137
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.000
set, there is a column called as house

138
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.000
size. Now, if that column contains some

139
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.000
negative values, then it is an error

140
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.000
because size of the house can never be

141
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.000
negative.

142
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Also, there can be some missing values

143
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.000
in that data set. There can be some

144
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.000
spelling mistakes. There can be some

145
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.000
unformatted text. All these problems are

146
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.000
removed in basic data cleaning.

147
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.000
Now, after basic data cleaning, the

148
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.000
fourth step is data splitting.

149
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.000
In the previous lectures, I have already

150
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.000
explained you very well the meaning of

151
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.000
data splitting.

152
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Data splitting, or we can say train test

153
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.000
split, is the process of dividing the

154
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.000
total available data into two parts.

155
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.000
The first part is the training data. The

156
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.000
second part is the testing data.

157
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.000
Training data is used by the machine

158
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.000
learning model to learn patterns and

159
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.000
features.

160
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.000
And testing data is used to evaluate the

161
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.000
performance of the machine learning

162
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
model because it is an unseen data.

163
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.000
Now, the training data is usually larger

164
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.000
than the testing data.

165
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Why?

166
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.000
Because training data is used by the

167
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.000
machine learning model to learn patterns

168
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.000
and features. That is why it is required

169
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.000
by machine learning model in larger

170
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.000
amounts.

171
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.000
But, testing data is used to evaluate

172
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.000
the performance of the machine learning

173
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.000
model. That is why it can be less.

174
00:07:03.000 --> 00:80:20.000
So, the standard data splitting ratio is

175
00:80:20.000 --> 00:07:08.000
.

176
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.000
That means 80% of the total available

177
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.000
data is training data, and 20% of the

178
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.000
total available data is used as testing

179
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.000
data.

180
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.000
Now, there is a one important point that

181
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.000
you should note.

182
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.000
Before performing the data splitting,

183
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.000
that is, before dividing the data into

184
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.000
training data and testing data, we must

185
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.000
first separate the features and labels.

186
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.000
For example, in case of house price data

187
00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.000
set, we must separate the house details

188
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.000
from the house price.

189
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.000
So, the house details like size of the

190
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.000
house, age of the house, number of

191
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.000
bedrooms of the house, location of the

192
00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.000
house must be separated from the actual

193
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.000
price of the house.

194
00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.000
Only after that we can perform data

195
00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.000
splitting, that is dividing the data

196
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:03.000
into training data and testing data.

197
00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.000
So, I hope data splitting is now clear

198
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.000
to you.

199
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.000
After that, the fifth step is data

200
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.000
preprocessing and feature engineering.

201
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.000
So, after data splitting, we need to

202
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.000
prepare the data. Now, data preparation

203
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.000
includes data preprocessing and feature

204
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.000
engineering. Let''s first talk about what

205
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.000
is data preprocessing.

206
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.000
Data preprocessing is the process of

207
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.000
converting the data into a format that

208
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.000
machine learning algorithm can

209
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.000
understand.

210
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.000
So, in data preprocessing, we convert

211
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.000
our data into a format that is

212
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.000
understandable by machine learning

213
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.000
algorithm. For example, a machine

214
00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.000
learning algorithm understands numeric

215
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.000
data better than textual data.

216
00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.000
Now, suppose there is a column in a data

217
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.000
set known as location, which contains

218
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.000
the values like Chennai, Mumbai, Agra,

219
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:02.000
and so on.

220
00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.000
Now, these textual values needs to be

221
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.000
converted into numerical values, so that

222
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.000
our machine learning algorithm can

223
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.000
understand them.

224
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.000
This is one of the examples of data

225
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.000
preprocessing. But, data preprocessing

226
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.000
is a very vast field.

227
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:23.000
It includes categorical encoding,

228
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.000
handling missing values, handling

229
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.000
outliers, feature scaling, data

230
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.000
transformation, and so on.

231
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.000
Don''t worry, I will explain you each of

232
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.000
these terms when we will understand data

233
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.000
preprocessing in details.

234
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.000
Now, the second one is feature

235
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.000
engineering.

236
00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.000
What is feature engineering?

237
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.000
It is the process of creating new

238
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.000
features from the existing features.

239
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:51.000
For example, there is a data set which

240
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.000
contains a column known as date of

241
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.000
birth.

242
00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.000
Now, if we create a new column called as

243
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.000
age from date of birth, then this is

244
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.000
known as feature engineering.

245
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.000
Obviously, age can be easily derived

246
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.000
from date of birth. So, we have created

247
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.000
a new feature that is age from the

248
00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.000
existing feature that is date of birth.

249
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.000
So, this is feature engineering.

250
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.000
And why feature engineering is

251
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.000
important?

252
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.000
Because it makes the data more

253
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.000
meaningful for the machine learning

254
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.000
algorithm.

255
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.000
So, now I hope you have a basic

256
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:26.000
understanding about data pre-processing

257
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.000
and feature engineering.

258
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.000
After that, the sixth step is selecting

259
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.000
algorithm. That means we need to select

260
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.000
an appropriate machine learning

261
00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.000
algorithm for the machine learning

262
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.000
model.

263
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.000
So, what is a machine learning

264
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.000
algorithm?

265
00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.000
Machine learning algorithm is the method

266
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.000
or approach used by machine learning

267
00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.000
model to learn patterns from the data.

268
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.000
I have explained you that machine

269
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.000
learning model learns patterns and

270
00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:57.000
features from the data.

271
00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.000
So, the approach or method used by the

272
00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.000
machine learning model to learn patterns

273
00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.000
from the data, that approach or method

274
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.000
is known as machine learning algorithm.

275
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.000
Now, the choice of machine learning

276
00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.000
algorithm depends on the type of

277
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:13.000
problem.

278
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:15.000
For example, in case of house price

279
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.000
prediction,

280
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.000
we need to predict the prices of the

281
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.000
houses, and price is a numerical value.

282
00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
That is why we choose a regression

283
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.000
algorithm like linear regression.

284
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.000
If we choose a wrong machine learning

285
00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:31.000
algorithm, the machine learning model

286
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.000
will give poor results

287
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.000
even if the quality of data is very

288
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:36.000
nice.

289
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.000
So, this was the step number six, that

290
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.000
is selecting a machine learning

291
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.000
algorithm.

292
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.000
So, till now we have defined the problem

293
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:47.000
statement, we have collected the data,

294
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.000
we have applied basic data cleaning,

295
00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.000
after that we have splitted the data

296
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:54.000
into two parts, training and testing.

297
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.000
After that we have prepared the data,

298
00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.000
that is data pre-processing and feature

299
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
engineering. And after that we have

300
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.000
selected an appropriate machine learning

301
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.000
algorithm.

302
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Now, it''s time to train the machine

303
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.000
learning model. So, the seventh step is

304
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.000
model training. In this step, the

305
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.000
machine learning model learns the

306
00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.000
patterns and features from the data.

307
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.000
Machine learning model learns the

308
00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.000
relationships between the feature and

309
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.000
target variable.

310
00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.000
For example, in a house price prediction

311
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:28.000
system, the linear regression model will

312
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.000
try to identify the relationships

313
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.000
between the house features and the house

314
00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.000
price. For example, if the size of the

315
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.000
house is bigger, the price would be

316
00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.000
higher. If the location of the house is

317
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.000
better, the price would be higher. Like

318
00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.000
this. So, this is known as model

319
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.000
training. Now, after model training, the

320
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.000
eighth step is model evaluation and

321
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.000
pipeline saving.

322
00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.000
So, what is model evaluation?

323
00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:00.000
Model evaluation means model testing.

324
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.000
That means, after training the machine

325
00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.000
learning model, we test the performance

326
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:08.000
of the machine learning model on unseen

327
00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.000
data.

328
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.000
In this way, we evaluate our machine

329
00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:14.000
learning model, how well it is trained

330
00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.000
on the training data set. So, model

331
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.000
training is done on the training data

332
00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:22.000
set and model evaluation, or we can say

333
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.000
model testing, is done on the testing

334
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:27.000
data set, which is unseen for the

335
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.000
machine learning model.

336
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:32.000
Now, after model evaluation, there is

337
00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.000
pipeline saving. What is the meaning of

338
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.000
pipeline saving?

339
00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:40.000
Now, after the model is evaluated, if it

340
00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.000
has a good performance, then we need to

341
00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:45.000
save the machine learning model.

342
00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.000
Why to save the machine learning model?

343
00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.000
So that we don''t need to retrain the

344
00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.000
machine learning model again and again

345
00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:53.000
from the beginning.

346
00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.000
This is known as saving a machine

347
00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:58.000
learning model, or I can also say saving

348
00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.000
the entire pipeline of machine learning.

349
00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.000
Now, after model evaluation and pipeline

350
00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.000
saving, the ninth step is making

351
00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.000
predictions.

352
00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:12.000
That means the trained, tested, and

353
00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.000
saved model is now used to make

354
00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.000
predictions on new data.

355
00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.000
And this is the actual practical use

356
00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.000
case of machine learning. All this we

357
00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:26.000
are doing just to ensure that we will

358
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.000
make predictions on the new data.

359
00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.000
Suppose a user enters the details like

360
00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.000
size of the house, location of the

361
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:36.000
house, age of the house, number of

362
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.000
bedrooms of the house, and so on. And

363
00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.000
model predicts the price of the house.

364
00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.000
This is known as making predictions on

365
00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.000
new data. And this is the actual use

366
00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.000
case of machine learning.

367
00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:53.000
Now, after making predictions, the last

368
00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:56.000
step is monitor and iterate.

369
00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.000
What is the meaning of monitor and

370
00:14:58.000 --> 00:14:59.000
iterate?

371
00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:01.000
It is to be noted that this machine

372
00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:04.000
learning pipeline does not end after

373
00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.000
making predictions.

374
00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:09.000
After making predictions, we need to

375
00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.000
keep on checking the performance of the

376
00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:13.000
machine learning model over the regular

377
00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.000
intervals of time.

378
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.000
So that we can check whether the machine

379
00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.000
learning model is still performing good

380
00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.000
or not.

381
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.000
This is known as monitoring of a machine

382
00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.000
learning model.

383
00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:29.000
And what is the meaning of iterate?

384
00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Iterate means to continuously improve

385
00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:33.000
the performance of the machine learning

386
00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.000
model.

387
00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:38.000
For example, the house prices may change

388
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.000
after few months.

389
00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.000
That is why we need to collect the new

390
00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.000
data, then we need to retrain the

391
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:45.000
machine learning model.

392
00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:49.000
This process is known as iteration. So,

393
00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:52.000
monitor and iterate means keep on

394
00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:53.000
checking the performance of the machine

395
00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.000
learning model and improving it wherever

396
00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:58.000
required.

397
00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.000
So, this is the entire machine learning

398
00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.000
pipeline that we have discussed.

399
00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.000
First, we define the problem statement,

400
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.000
then we collect the data, then we clean

401
00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:11.000
the data, after that we split the data

402
00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.000
into training and testing data,

403
00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:16.000
after that we prepare the data, that is

404
00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.000
data pre-processing and feature

405
00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:20.000
engineering. After that, we select an

406
00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.000
appropriate machine learning algorithm.

407
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.000
After that, we train the machine

408
00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.000
learning model, then we test and save

409
00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.000
the machine learning model. After that,

410
00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:31.000
we use the machine learning model to

411
00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:34.000
make predictions on new data, and we

412
00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.000
continuously monitor and iterate the

413
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.000
performance of the machine learning

414
00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:39.000
model.

415
00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:42.000
In this way, we are done with our topic

416
00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.000
machine learning pipeline.

417
00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
In the upcoming lectures, we will

418
00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.000
understand each of the steps of this ML

419
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.000
pipeline in more details.

420
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.000
So, this was all from my side for this

421
00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.000
lecture. Thank you, and I''ll see you in

