00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.000
while data structures gives 
us the implementation.

31
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
But, how to know which data 
structure to use for a particular ADT?

32
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.000
That is the basic question.

33
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Obviously, this question immediately arises

34
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.000
after knowing this point that, there are

35
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.000
multiple data structures for a particular ADT.

36
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.000
But, which data structure to use

37
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
for a particular ADT is the question of concern.

38
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Let''s tackle this question.

39
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Which data structure?

40
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
In reality, different implementations of ADT

41
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.000
are compared for time and space efficiency.

42
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.000
For example, we know that stack ADT

43
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
can be implemented using arrays or linked lists.

44
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
We can compare these two data structures,

45
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.000
for time and space efficiency.

46
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
The one best suited according to the

47
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
current requirement of the user will be selected.

48
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.000
May be one data structure will lead to

49
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
space efficiency while other data structure

50
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.000
will lead to time efficiency.

51
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
We will select the one according to

52
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
the current requirement of the user.

53
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.000
May be user wants to save space,

54
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.000
then we can use a particular data structure

55
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.000
which can save space. May be a 
user wants run the programmer faster,

56
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
then we will use the data structure,

57
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.000
which will run faster.

58
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.000
So, the one best suited according to the

59
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.000
current requirement of the 
user will get selected. Okay.

60
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.000
So, it is depending upon the user.

61
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Now, let''s discuss some of the 
advantages of data structures.

62
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.000
First advantage is efficiency.

63
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.000
We know that, proper choice of data structure

64
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.000
make program efficient in 
terms of space and time.

65
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Proper choice of data 
structure is very important

66
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.000
This leads to space and time efficiency.

67
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Second advantage is reusability.

68
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.000
One implementation can be 
used by multiple client programs.

69
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Just one data structure can be 
used by multiple client programs.

70
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.000
So, re-usability is also provided 
by data structures. Right?

71
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Apart from this, the one advantage

72
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.000
which we already know is abstraction.

73
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.000
Data structure is specified by an ADT.

74
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
ADT is the blueprint, while data 
structure is the implementation.

75
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.000
We can say that, it provides a 
level of abstraction. Right?

76
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.000
The client program doesn''t have 
to worry about what is going inside.

77
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
It just sees the ADT, just 
the interface, nothing else.

78
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.000
So, these are the advantages of

79
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
data structures which we should remember. Okay.

80
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Okay friends, this is it for now.

81
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Thank you for watching this presentation.
