SELECT si.id, '# Study Guide: Defining Member Functions in C++

## Conceptual Overview
Lecture explains how to define member functions in C++ either outside the class using the scope resolution operator or inside the class (as inline functions), including access to private members and differences in behavior between the two approaches.

Defining member functions outside the class using scope resolution operator: specify return type, class name, scope operator, and function name.
Outside definitions can access private members of the class.
Defining member functions inside the class makes them inline and potentially optimized by the compiler.


## Key Terminology & Concepts
- **C++**: Foundational concept covered in this lecture.
- **Object-Oriented Programming**: Foundational concept covered in this lecture.
- **Member Functions**: Foundational concept covered in this lecture.
- **Scope Resolution Operator**: Foundational concept covered in this lecture.
- **Inline Functions**: Foundational concept covered in this lecture.

## Supplementary Reading & Official Reference
For further practice and deep-dive technical examples, consult the official study material reference:
- [Defining Member Functions in C++ - In-Depth Documentation & Examples](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/java/object-oriented-programming-oops-concept-in-java/)

## Review Questions for Self-Assessment
1. What is the primary purpose and syntax of Defining Member Functions in C++?
2. How does the compiler or runtime handle edge cases for this construct?
3. In what scenarios should this pattern be used over alternative implementations?', 65
